  "problem": [
    {
      "text": "After LDLQ produces quantized weights and trellis indices, `quantize_exl3` computes a Hessian-weighted diagnostic. The parent evaluates two full-width contractions, one for the quantization residual and one for the reference weights. A `torch.OutOfMemoryError` at this stage leaves the diagnostic unavailable even if quantization itself has finished.",
      "basis": "source",
      "evidence": ["call_f5d9b07104f741f0a7e78868f1fe5b8b"]
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": [
    {
      "text": "The patch partitions the contracted input-channel index rather than taking only diagonal Hessian blocks. Every Hessian column is used exactly once, and each block still sees every Hessian row. This preserves cross-block contributions and the full metric in exact arithmetic.",
      "basis": "source",
      "evidence": ["call_18f1e39716ea46e8b1559d14a81813ef"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Reducing the size of contraction intermediates is a plausible reason to chunk the diagnostic: contracting the full weight operand with a Hessian column slice first would produce an intermediate with at most 1,024 columns along the sliced axis. The actual intermediate shapes depend on contraction order. The tradeoff is more contractions and scalar reads; on CUDA, each `.item()` needs its partial result before the Python loop can continue.",
      "basis": "inference",
      "evidence": ["call_18f1e39716ea46e8b1559d14a81813ef", "call_f5d9b07104f741f0a7e78868f1fe5b8b"]
    }
  ],
  "impact": [
    {
      "text": "Only the post-quantization metric calculation changes. The LDLQ search, reconstruction, and packing are untouched. Conversion still displays a nonnegative score to six decimal places or `(OoM)` for the negative sentinel. The repository accuracy test still does not distinguish that sentinel from a score below its threshold.",
      "basis": "source",
      "evidence": ["call_18f1e39716ea46e8b1559d14a81813ef", "call_f5d9b07104f741f0a7e78868f1fe5b8b", "call_2a2ee06adc5e4f89bd9e8be310d08797", "call_48397237040a4969948cbe900cdef310"]
    }
  ],
  "source_notes": [
    {
      "text": "The original quantizer chapter describes the transformed-weight space and why the normalized Hessian proxy should not be treated as the original activation loss. The earlier metric-OoM entry describes the `-1.0` fallback and records the test's upper-bound-only check.",
